conomy of of  movement of actorly things very  economical  um always comes across as a very uh  deep well-rounded character  um and you can also sense that  whatever what a good person he is um  and that's what was a delight every day  he's fun  he knows what it what it takes to make a  movie  uh the work is hard um but he was  more than well prepared each day to  shoot what we needed to shoot  and uh you realize you know in the end  this  has got to be a good time we're going to  do good work but  you know he was an actor's director  and a director's actor that you know  couldn't ask for a better one best in  both worlds  um there's this fight scene that the two  of you have  that uh well a fight scene maybe in  quotes it kind of devolves into like  a silly sort of laughter filled  wrestling match what was the process of  staging that scene executing that scene  were there ever any versions where like  it was a little more intense  what was that yeah it was say okay let's  go for this we're sitting on a couch  and okay you're gonna get up here and  then i'm gonna push you and then i'm  gonna tackle you and get on the ground  and let's just do it and it was  it was hilarious we were laughing  sincerely laughing our asses off because  this uh  but you know in retrospect i think  it was important to establish the bond  between these two guys  you know the friendship the trust and  also the boundaries  um and uh eddie just said i think we got  to go for it  and and we did and i it stuck out in my  mind as being one of the more  uh enjoyable days um  and also a really significant moment in  the journey of both  characters um had you not had that scene  i think you'd still have the  relationship but that  at the get-go laid the groundwork for um  for the depth of the relationship yeah  um  what do you think is going on deep  within  fred your character what is sort of the  the central  emotion or drive that kind of uh  uh decides his decisions his emotions  a couple of things uh they touch on the  fact that his wife  has died he's by himself you know he's  he's hoping to get out of the game he  has his dream of a sailboat  and maybe retiring from you know the  arduous work of  of maintaining that his um his his  business concerns  the almonds and uh  so there's that bubbling is he  going to actually enter in  engage in this battle given  all of that um how much does he care  uh his love of his almonds  and uh and then when he's  awakened to the  to what shore oil is doing i i think  that  triggered a um  a defense of of the land a defense  of uh not only his business  but uh this is wrong they're poisoning  the earth  um i am or as fred the farmer i am  providing  food for the world they're poisoning my  food  and so on a very you know  very basic level uh  i think that got to him and said yes  these people deserve and when he learned  about how much money they're  stealing from him well just on a you  know business sense that that was uh you  know that  that certainly made him get fired up  um speaking of poisoning the water maybe  this is an odd question  there's that scene where your  character's taking a shower and he kind  of realizes  he even tastes the water and says oh  it's the water  as an actor when you are playing a scene  where you realize water is unhealthy and  water  is coming down on you is there a part of  your brain that starts to think  is this water literally coming down on  me safe  does that sort of like start to collide  or anything like that  uh yeah i think that was the intention  of the scene  is a kind of a wake-up call to what we  take for granted  that the water coming out of our faucets  is uh  is good for us and clean and healthy and  pure  and etc etc you know think of flint  michigan  you know think of erin brockovich think  of  you know what the groundwater  may be affected by the fires in northern  california  anywhere there has been well across the  midwest with the um  roundup and all of the poisons that  putting into the soil  and here coming out of a shower head is  potentially  a a toxic source which we  consider or well reconsider which is  vital to  survival on the planet so that's the  intention of the scene it's brief it's a  brief moment  but you picked it off that's what eddie  was hoping to achieve in that moment  yeah um there's a  not soon after that scene there's a  pretty wild scene where you  are cladding just a towel and a golf  club  and you go and smash the heck out of a  window of a hummer and it looks like  it's in one take  yeah how was that yeah did you actually  like smash  a window out of a hummer yeah yeah we we  kind of went through the  the steps you know the process but said  well we  we we got to go we did try a couple of  takes but i realized  oh i'm not going to be able to hit it we  better i'm not going to break the window  you know okay stop we'll do it again but  um  yeah that was uh that was a tricky  little  thing to to accomplish um but  i i think it's one of those scenes in  the film a lot like the wrestling scene  where  this is hilarious this looks like you  know some kind of  cartoon weird satire comedy  you know mad comedy but the um the  intensity  the the motivation for that i think  is part and parcel of the whole  litigation um this is he finally  realizes that he's been  has been taken he's sort of been  betrayed he's been duped scammed  and that kind of passion  was i think was necessary to see  in the character which would propel him  forward into taking the case  yeah that makes a lot of sense uh i  think i have time for one more question  uh we'll do a silly one for you in this  film  i think we have three very  exquisite pieces of facial hair there's  your beard there's edward james almost  this beard and then there's haley joel  osment's beard  uh if you had to rank those beards  best of worst what do we got what's your  power ranking  uh well obviously haley's was younger  and fresher and fuller  um but i'd take eddie's  this is number one yeah i take care of  his number one  and then you've got to be number two  come on well  uh it was a little scant and you know a  little  it was more like the almond grove after  poison for a while  awesome i think that's all the time i  have thanks david all right  thanks greg

